# bmc-ui-*.tar.gz is a GitHub RELEASE ASSET, not a tag archive: a release
# asset can be re-uploaded under the same name, and GitHub exposes no digest
# for it. Pinned on purpose -- if the asset ever changes, the build fails here
# instead of silently shipping a different UI.
#
# This is our fork's release, not upstream's. Upstream v3.3.7 rendered
# "vundefined" for the build version, reported the firmware name as the
# Buildroot release, shipped 668.9 KB of fonts including 21 .woff files no
# browser that can run the bundle needs, and -- despite bmc-ui.mk declaring
# BMC_UI_LICENSE_FILES = LICENSE -- shipped no LICENSE at all, which for a
# GPL-2.0 work we redistribute was a real gap. Ours carries it.
#
# Verified 2026-09-11: 577222 bytes, built by GitHub Actions from tag
# v3.21.0. The sum was recomputed from the downloaded bytes rather than read
# out of the release's own SHA256SUMS -- a manifest published beside an asset
# cannot attest to it, and catching a re-uploaded asset is the whole reason
# this file exists. It does agree with that manifest, which is a cross-check
# and not the source. The tarball carries dist/LICENSE, and the grid class
# the Nodes page needs is present in the built bundle -- both checked on the
# downloaded archive, not assumed from the tag.
#
# v3.16.0 folded the pages' standing explanations behind (i) popovers that
# link to the docs site. v3.17.0 removed the Metrics token card: /metrics
# moved to its own credential-free listener, so there is no token to show or
# rotate, and a card offering to rotate one would imply a protection that is
# not there. v3.18.0 stops hand-writing the API's response types: twenty-three
# interfaces are now aliases onto a file generated from the openapi.json that
# bmcd publishes, and CI regenerates and diffs so a hand edit or a pin moved
# without regenerating fails there rather than on a board. v3.19.0 removes
# the maskrom warning from the flash page: SQU-105 landed and the claim it
# made -- that a flash hits whichever module answered first -- was disproved
# on the board with two modules in maskrom at once. v3.20.0 gives the Nodes
# page a two-by-two grid -- four full-width stacked rows scrolled on a 1080p
# screen with a third of the width empty -- and adds the demo mode that
# answers from captured fixtures, which is what turingpi.xyz serves. v3.21.0
# tightens that grid so it also fits the demo's frame: on a card wider than a
# phone the controls and the two name fields share a row again, which is
# upstream's own arrangement that the first grid had stacked.
#
# The sum is UNCHANGED across the move to excavador-turing. The asset was
# rebuilt from the same tree under the new owner and came out byte-identical
# -- 573360 bytes, same sha256 -- because the release workflow packs with
# `tar --sort=name | gzip -9 -n`, which pins member order and drops the
# gzip timestamp. Only the URL in bmc-ui.mk changed.
# v3.23.0 -> v3.24.0: the firmware install confirmation is a modal.
#
# It used to render inline, after every candidate. A board with several
# sources has a long catalogue, so pressing INSTALL scrolled the question off
# the bottom of the window -- the button appeared to do nothing, and the
# obvious response is to press it again. It now uses the same modal the reboot
# and node-power confirmations use, whose drawer form also fixes the phone
# case the inline panel was worst on. Reported from use, 2026-09-11.
#
# Measured 2026-09-11: `make bmc-ui-source` reported got 7beaafb3 against a
# zeroed expectation, then re-run to confirm it fetches and verifies cleanly.
# v3.24.0 -> v3.25.0: choose an image off the SD card, and two bugs that
# were sitting in the flash path.
#
# Both flash forms read `form.elements.namedItem("file-url")`, and no form
# has had that field for several releases: `namedItem` answers null, `.value`
# throws, and the handler died inside the confirmation modal -- so pressing
# Install did NOTHING, every time, with the TypeError visible only in the
# browser console. The firmware tab's candidate list is the path people
# actually use, which is why it went unreported.
#
# `GET /api/bmc/sdcard` answers an ARRAY of one, not an object. Read as an
# object it gives an undefined `free`, which reaches `filesize` and throws
# "Invalid number", blanking the tab behind an error boundary.
#
# The feature: the picker lists what is on the board's card, with sizes, and
# says which entries can be written to a module -- `flashable` and `reason`
# as the daemon sends them, never a filename check in the interface. Measured
# on bmc-1 through a browser: 16 entries, 2 flashable, 14 refused with
# reasons like "BMC firmware, not a node image".
#
# Measured 2026-09-12: 578647 bytes, downloaded from the release and summed
# from those bytes. It agrees with the release's own SHA256SUMS, which is a
# cross-check and not the source -- a manifest published beside an asset
# cannot attest to it. dist/LICENSE is present, and the picker's own string
# is in the built bundle, so the release carries the feature it claims.
# v3.25.0 -> v3.26.0: the temperature moves to Board Health, and the console
# stops blaming the wrong thing.
#
# Board Health showed uptime, load, memory, NAND and the clock, and the board's
# temperature was two tabs away on Settings inside the fan card -- a READING
# filed with a CONTROL. It is a row there now, with the fan's step and the trip
# that explains it: "53.8 °C  fan step 5 of 6  above the 45 °C trip", read off
# bmc-1. The slider stays on Settings.
#
# The console's explanation named a rejected session token and a daemon too old
# to serve the endpoint. Measured on a fresh login, the browser reports
# net::ERR_SSL_PROTOCOL_ERROR on the WebSocket while every other tab works --
# a browser will not open one to a certificate it does not trust, and accepting
# the page's warning does not extend to it. The hint leads with that now.
#
# Measured 2026-09-12: 579793 bytes, summed from the downloaded asset. It
# agrees with the release's own SHA256SUMS, which is a cross-check and not the
# source. dist/LICENSE is present and the new strings are in the built bundle.
# v3.26.0 -> v3.27.0: the fleet's console connects, and Settings can say who
# may get in.
#
# The console refused before opening a socket, because it demanded a bmcd
# session token the fleet has no reason to hold -- every other tab on the same
# page was answering while that one said "there is no session token". The
# certificate was never involved: the browser has always talked to its own
# origin and never to a board. Holding a token and being able to open a socket
# are different questions; through the gateway the browser presents nothing and
# the identity is attached on a hop it is not part of. Proved against bmc-1
# through a stand-in for Envoy: 101 Switching Protocols, subprotocol
# bmcd.serial.v1, and "connected" in headless Chrome with zero console errors.
#
# The access card needs bmcd 2.36.0. On an older daemon it hides itself -- and
# getting that right needed a real older board: bmcd serves the web interface
# from the same listener as the API and falls back to index.html for anything
# The version is v3.28.0: a reconnect stops writing a second copy of the
# scrollback. Pressing Reconnect keeps the terminal, as it is meant to, and
# then replayed the daemon's whole 16 KiB ring buffer underneath what was
# already on screen -- the same lines with the same kernel timestamps, twice.
# Reported from a phone on 2026-09-12 against the fleet AND against a board's
# own interface, which is one component serving both, so this pin is what
# carries the fix to the board.
#
# The replay writes only the part of the buffer the terminal has not already
# been given. Clearing first would have fixed it and cost the scrollback,
# which is the only place a full boot survives: the daemon keeps 16 KiB and a
# boot is about 82 KB, measured on board B the same day.
#
# Measured 2026-09-12: 582029 bytes, summed from the downloaded asset. It
# agrees with the release's own SHA256SUMS, which is a cross-check and not the
# source. dist/LICENSE is present.
#
# CORRECTED. This note first claimed the console chunk contained the NUL
# separator the overlap is built on. It does not, and the check that said so
# was worthless: `grep -l ... | head && echo found` reports the exit status of
# `head`, which is 0 whether or not grep matched anything. The minifier folds
# `String.fromCharCode(0)` away, so the marker never survives a build -- a
# build of the same source on this workstation does not contain it either.
#
# What the release does carry, established by comparing it against v3.27.0:
# the console chunk differs and is 605 bytes larger, and the only commit
# between the two tags is the fix. Size and provenance, not a string match.
#
# The version is v3.29.0, and it carries two things a fleet operator could not
# see. A toast now names the board it came from: with two boards on one screen
# "Flashing started" told you an operation began somewhere. And a node armed
# for USB boot says so on its own liveness line -- a module whose USB-boot pin
# is held will not boot from its own eMMC, and the reboot that reveals it can
# be weeks after whatever armed it, by which point it looks exactly like dead
# hardware.
#
# Measured 2026-09-13: 582709 bytes, summed from the downloaded asset. It
# agrees with the release's own SHA256SUMS, which is a cross-check and not the
# source. dist/LICENSE is present, 49 entries.
#
# Proved the artifact actually carries both changes, in BOTH directions --
# the previous note on this file had to be corrected for claiming a check that
# never ran, so this one states what was executed. All six locale strings for
# the USB-boot warning are present under dist/assets ("armed for USB boot",
# "preparado para arranque USB", "USB-Boot scharf", "gereed voor USB-boot",
# "uzbrojony do rozruchu", and the zh-Hans form) and every one of them is
# ABSENT from v3.28.0. Locale strings are data, so they survive minification;
# that is why this check works where a grep for a folded-away
# String.fromCharCode(0) did not.
#
# The toast change is code, not a string, so it shows as chunk size instead:
# against v3.28.0 the `use` chunk goes 311 -> 7072 bytes (use-toast gained the
# scope), `nodes.lazy` 10997 -> 11375 (the liveness line), `index` 528264 ->
# 529645.
sha256  00c0107a98921fc496ec2883882b767480a53907d99d01322f6383c9b9c81d8e  bmc-ui-v3.30.0.tar.gz
#
# v3.30.0 -> v3.32.0, two releases. v3.31.0 is the fleet's own: it says which
# version of itself it is (the Dockerfile exported the tag without the VITE_
# prefix Vite reads, so the value arrived at the build and stopped there),
# gains the footer the board interface already had, and points its docs link
# at turingpi.xyz. Nothing of it reaches a board's bundle, which is why no
# firmware pinned it. v3.32.0 does: Console, USB and Flash each opened with a
# drop-down that unfolded to show four fixed modules, one already chosen. The
# three tabs now share one NodePicker -- Node 1 to 4 side by side, the chosen
# one green, a radio group for the keyboard. Asked for from the fleet's
# console on 2026-09-21; it is one component serving both, so this pin is
# what carries it to the board.
#
# Measured 2026-09-21: 600069 bytes, downloaded from the release and summed
# from those bytes. It agrees with the release's own SHA256SUMS, which is a
# cross-check and not the source. dist/LICENSE is present, 53 entries. The
# picker is shared code, so it lands in the index chunk rather than a tab's:
# the string "radio" is in dist/assets/index-DifQtqNw.js of this archive,
# which is the role the pills carry.
sha256  bd64279ebaceaf4f69c921747e94248de0e405c2d6c92906db98219100ed19f8  bmc-ui-v3.32.0.tar.gz
#
# v3.32.0 -> v3.34.1, three releases. v3.33.0 is the fleet's own (a fresh
# board's first login lands on the password change, not the switch card) and
# nothing of it reaches a board's bundle. v3.34.0 is the release this pin is
# for: an Address card beside Hostname on the Network tab -- DHCP or static,
# every rule the board's via /network/address/validate, Apply and Try it as
# on the switch card, kept only when the reloaded page confirms from the new
# address -- and a Time card that prints chrony's verdict per source,
# including "unresolved" for the board with no resolver that started all of
# this. Also: "Reset network" renamed to "Reset the switch chip", the Time and
# Hostname boxes no longer blank on a second visit, and a countdown that had
# been reading the daemon's {secs_since_epoch, nanos_since_epoch} as a date.
# v3.34.1 fixes the demo build only: v3.34.0's captured.json was not JSON
# because the capture script wanted a `v` on a version that read `local`.
# The board bundle is unaffected, but v3.34.0 cannot be pinned by a site that
# builds the demo from the same tag, and the rule is one pin for both.
#
# Measured 2026-09-21: 606668 bytes, downloaded from the release and summed
# from those bytes. It agrees with the release's own SHA256SUMS, which is a
# cross-check and not the source. dist/LICENSE is present, 53 entries. The
# new strings are in the built bundle: "unresolved" in
# settings.lazy-CRLqjvDH.js and index-CUJYxzOR.js, "Reset the switch chip"
# and "Try it" each in one chunk -- locale strings are data, so they survive
# minification, which is why this check works where a folded-away constant
# would not.
sha256  fac5b37399ac09c9efc21a2e67e11d1a76a8cf19254fe54e105c095d09495eb0  bmc-ui-v3.34.1.tar.gz
#
# v3.34.1 -> v3.35.0: the site's demo learned the address flow -- apply, a
# window, confirm, revert -- from an in-memory stand-in for the board, so a
# reader can run the sequence instead of reading about it. NOTHING of it
# reaches a board: the simulation lives under src/demo/ and ships only with
# VITE_DEMO=1. Pinned here because the pin gate requires the latest release,
# not because a board gains anything; v3.33.0 was the same case.
#
# Measured 2026-09-22: 606680 bytes, downloaded from the release and summed
# from those bytes. It agrees with the release's own SHA256SUMS, which is a
# cross-check and not the source. dist/LICENSE is present, 53 entries. The
# demo's strings ("stand-in for the board", DemoAddress, verdictFor) are
# ABSENT from every chunk under dist/assets -- checked on the downloaded
# archive -- which is the claim above, tested rather than assumed.
sha256  e77c62b69853e4e205401a7a331e8ff91afada3957736d6a3f416305590e9578  bmc-ui-v3.35.0.tar.gz
#
# v3.35.0 -> v3.36.0: the password form would not accept typing, in any of
# its three boxes. The shared Input dropped the caller's onChange for
# type="password", and a controlled React field with `value` and no way to
# report a change is read-only -- every keystroke reverted, silently, with
# markup that looks perfect. It hit the password card on Access AND the
# factory-password page a board shows before it will do anything else, so a
# board still on its shipped password could not be taken off it from a
# browser at all. Login was unaffected because it reads the DOM on submit
# rather than holding state, which is why it survived since the Tailwind
# move. Reported as BMC-Firmware#48 by a user on v2.34.0.
#
# The release also adds the gate that would have caught it: `npm run
# type-test` drives the demo over the DevTools protocol, types into every
# visible box on every tab and reads the value back. Nothing that existed
# could see this -- it builds, it lints, it renders, and the screens gate
# measures height.
#
# Measured 2026-09-22: 606681 bytes, downloaded from the release and summed
# from those bytes. It agrees with the release's own SHA256SUMS, which is a
# cross-check and not the source. dist/LICENSE is present, 53 entries. The
# password form's own field name is in the built bundle
# (index-tyBdLgum.js), so the release carries the form this fixes.
sha256  dcff2283b33b58d87d749e3f3e9c9877c5b020cd23fbbfdee063fe764130562c  bmc-ui-v3.36.0.tar.gz
#
# v3.36.0 -> v3.37.0: installing a firmware image says what it is doing, and
# for how long. The daemon does the whole install inside one request --
# download, sum, write to the spare slot, arm -- and the page showed a
# greyed button for the whole of that; a reader on Discord wondered whether
# to refresh. Now the button reads "Installing..." and a status line gives
# "about 26 s, now at N", the number measured on board B (a real install of
# v2.37.0 from GitHub, 26 s wall, 2026-09-23), the same shape as the reboot
# banner. Interface only; the daemon is unchanged.
#
# Measured 2026-09-23: 607006 bytes, downloaded from the release and summed
# from those bytes, agreeing with the release's own SHA256SUMS as a
# cross-check. dist/LICENSE present, 53 entries.
sha256  3da2669bc0cdaf7dd1d0a846ce967b3465409914a4ad28d6c4d87df8177cefff  bmc-ui-v3.37.0.tar.gz
#
# v3.37.0 -> v3.38.0: a refused validate request shows the daemon's words
